        {
          "id": 1224410198,
          "type": "DiffNote",
          "body": "Also note this patch should have changed the ASN.1 that the dissector is generated from, and it just got largely erased by !9252.\n\n`conversation_set_dissector()` is used in situations where the default port-based configuration wouldn't cause a dissector to be called first. Mostly that's heuristic dissection (which SNMP doesn't have), but it can be situations where the current packet is going to set up a conversation about some other ports than the current pair, such NBAP setting up a UMTS_FP conversation on different ports, or TFTP setting up a wildcarded conversation on different ports because the initial packet is sent to the TFTP port from a random port, and then the server switches to a random port. A variation on heuristic dissection is when it's used to help avoid rejecting packets that are continuation data from TCP fragmentation. (There's a few edge cases where it's useful involving both ports being IANA-assigned or configured for different dissectors.)\n\nFor UDP, setting a conversation dissector is particularly wrong if the conversation isn't all the same packet type. The original code was a bit wrong but the needed fix is a bit different.",
          "author": {
            "id": 6764551,
            "username": "johnthacker",
            "public_email": "",
            "name": "John Thacker",
            "state": "active",
            "locked": false,
            "avatar_url": "https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/99c30adacedf45514aaf711ba8a44f56322ebb53e9b550b7e3c9cd83bd6a1408?s=80&d=identicon",
            "web_url": "https://gitlab.com/johnthacker"
          },
